    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I know if I need foundation repair?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The most common warning signs are: diagonal cracks in drywall near door frames or windows, doors or windows that stick or won't latch properly, floors that slope or feel uneven, gaps forming between walls and the ceiling or floor, and cracks in exterior brick or mortar joints." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How long does foundation repair take?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Most residential slab repairs in Tyler are completed in one to two days. The number of piers needed determines the timeline — a small 4-pier job might be done in a single day, while a larger 12–15 pier project typically takes two days." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Will my homeowner's insurance cover foundation repair?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "In most cases, standard Texas homeowner's insurance does not cover foundation repair caused by soil movement or settling. Insurance typically only covers foundation damage caused by a sudden covered event like a burst pipe." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do foundation repairs come with a warranty?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes — most licensed foundation repair contractors in our network offer a lifetime transferable warranty on their pier work. If you sell your home, the warranty transfers to the new owner." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What's the difference between slab and pier-and-beam foundations?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "A slab foundation is a single continuous pour of concrete that your home sits directly on — common in Tyler homes built after 1960. A pier-and-beam foundation raises the home on wooden beams supported by piers, leaving a crawl space underneath — typical in Tyler's older historic neighborhoods." }
    }
  ]
}
</script>
